Files in this folder are based on libxml2 2.15.4.

Original source: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/libxml2, tag v2.15.4.

Only the library is kept: its sources (and libxml.h and timsort.h) in src/,
the generated tables they include in src/codegen/, the private headers in
src/include/private/ (where they find src/libxml.h), the public headers in
include/libxml/, and Copyright and README.md in docs/, renamed COPYING and
README. xmllint, xmlcatalog, the tests, the Python bindings and the build
files are left out, and so are nanohttp.c (HTTP support was removed in
2.15) and xmlmodule.c (module loading is off).

include/libxml/xmlversion.h is made from xmlversion.h.in with the features
LiveCode's 2.9.4 build had, less HTTP, FTP and modules (removed or off);
src/config.h is written for OXT-Beyond's build (gyp runs no configure
step).

Eight local changes, marked "OXT-Beyond" in the sources:

- From LiveCode (bug 7586): xmlSAX2StartElementNoNS in src/SAX2.c
  (declared in include/libxml/SAX2.h), used by revXML when namespace
  processing is off. It is xmlSAX2StartElement without splitting element
  and attribute names at their prefix.
- In src/parser.c, a parser with a SAX1 handler (revXML's) passes the
  start tag's line to xmlParseEndTag1, so "Opening and ending tag
  mismatch: b line 2 and c" names the line, as 2.9 did, not line 0.
- In src/xpath.c, xmlXPathParseLiteral takes a byte that is not UTF-8
  as part of the literal, as 2.9 did, instead of refusing the expression.
  revXML passes text in the native encoding (Windows-1252, ISO-8859-1)
  and keeps native text's bytes in the trees it parses, so an XPath
  literal in native text matches them.
- In src/xmlIO.c (xmlSerializeText) and src/encoding.c
  (xmlCharEncOutput), a byte that is not UTF-8 is written as a reference
  to the ISO-8859-1 character it is ("&#xE9;"), where 2.15 wrote
  "&#xFFFD;" or ended the output. Such bytes are native text that
  revXML keeps in its trees.
- In xmlSetDeclaredEncoding in src/parserInternals.c, a document that
  declares UTF-16 but reaches its declaration in 8-bit characters (no
  byte order mark) is an error, "Document labelled UTF-16 but has UTF-8
  content", and is read on as UTF-8, as 2.9 did. 2.15 switched to UTF-16
  there and read the rest as garbage, which left revXML an empty tree.
- In src/HTMLtree.c, the HTML output that revXML's XSLT functions return
  is 2.9's again. htmlSetMetaEncoding, which libxslt's html and xhtml
  output methods call, follows 2.9's rules: the head's first <meta
  http-equiv="Content-Type" content="..."> gets "text/html; charset=..."
  unless it names the encoding already, a <meta charset="..."> is left as
  it is, and where there is no such meta it adds <meta
  http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=...">, not
  HTML5's <meta charset="..."> (as does the serializer, when it adds one).
  And an attribute value holding a double quote and no single quote is
  written in single quotes, alt='x"y', not as alt="x&quot;y".
- In src/dict.c, xmlInitRandom falls back to its seed from the time and
  some addresses when getentropy fails with an error other than EINTR (a
  sandbox that refuses the getrandom system call fails it with EPERM) or
  BCryptGenRandom fails, where 2.15 aborts the process. The seed only
  spreads the hash tables.

LiveCode's other changes to 2.9.4 are not needed: revXML now parses
through a SAX parser context with xmlCtxtReadMemory and xmlCtxtReadFile
instead of the two parse functions LiveCode added, and the rest were
fixes for old compilers. The parse is not quite the same as 2.9's: with
XML_PARSE_HUGE, 2.15 still refuses elements nested more than 2048 deep and
text nodes, attribute values or names over 1,000,000,000 bytes, where 2.9
had no limit, and it reads a gzip-compressed file only with
XML_PARSE_UNZIP, which revXML passes for files.
